In article <395526A5.15E9AD2B@attglobal.net>, Dale Stevenson <Dale.Stevenson@attglobal.net> writes:
:I have five standalone VAX 7820s and 4705s in three states.

  I will assume OpenVMS, but that isn't central to the question...

:Digital :has told me that they have heard of users connecting the system 
:consoles to a DecServer to allow easy remote support.  I have DS90M 
:terminal servers at all sites.

  Yep, we use this configuration quite regularly here in Compaq
  OpenVMS Engineering -- we can connect to services on the DECserver
  which are wired to the particular console port.  Works just fine.

  I'll assume you have an extended LAN (bridged or routed) connecting
  these systems, and that you do not wish to use a (password-protected 
  and/or dial-back) modem or other technique to permit relatively secure 
  remote console access.

:Does anyone have the details of how to achieve remote console operations
:in this situation?

  Typically, you need to get familiar with how to send a BREAK (or if
  you even can send one), and how you can control access to the port 
  from the DECserver, including security and configuration settings.

  The baud rate and the settings on most consoles are fixed -- see the 
  OpenVMS FAQ for details on this topic.  Lock the DECserver serial
  line port into this configuration -- usually something like 9600
  8N1, though a few OpenVMS systems do have console hardware port 
  speed selection capabilities.

  You will also potentially have the occasional framing error if
  something "burps" on the serial line like the DECserver powering
  up or powering down -- a framing error is indistinguishable from
  a BREAK -- and please see the FAQ for details on why this can be 
  bad, particularly when you are connected to a console that is set 
  up to process BREAK signals as a HALT.  Some systems -- a very
  few, and mostly the older VAX systems -- can select (or will use)
  CTRL/P as the console HALT request.

  You also have to be careful with grounding and such, to prevent
  current flow on the serial line wiring -- once upon a time, there
  was a set of opto-isolators available (the FCOC stuff, IIRC) for 
  just this sort of situation.  I suspect these couplers are still
  available from other sources.

  Security is another concern, given what you can do with the console
  serial line.

 --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
   Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




