Taco Buitenhuis wrote:
> 
> We had to rebuild a digital cluster and now the failover takes 5-10 minutes.
> This time used to be less than 1 minute. Does anyone know a solution to
> speed up the failover?

Assuming it's a unix cluster :

Check your customized scripts. If they've been altered it could be them.
If you don't find anything, comment out your customized scripts and do a
clean failover. Is it still slow ?

Be sure that your network cards & switch doesn't do AutoNegotiation.
Lock them to a fixed speed and simplex/duplex.

Check your log files /var/adm/syslog.dated/current/daemon.log &
kern.log. They might have some info about the failover and what goes
wrong.

While failing over, do a constant check (or copy) of the PID files i
/var/ase/tmp. These textfiles contain the stderr/stdout output from the
scripts/programs run by ASE in a failover situation.

Do a ps -ef |grep ase to see what's being run by ASE. This should give
you the process(es) taking the time.


l8r/Jspr



