In article <8it7d2$8hb$1@bob.news.rcn.net>, "Mike Duffy" <mdduffy@erols.com> writes:
:Doesn't DU use the other (not-SRM) console, ARC?

  Tru64 UNIX and OpenVMS both typically use the SRM console.

  AFAIK, to get Tru64 UNIX to boot, you must switch to the SRM console.

  Only the Microsoft operating system platform uses ARC or AlphaBIOS.
  (The ARC and AlphaBIOS both present the hardware abstraction layer
  (HAL) interface that is required for use with Microsoft Windows NT.)

:Joe Comunale wrote in message <3950EF83.48A5574B@forbin.qc.edu>...
:>My VX-40 runs Debian Linux. On bootup I saw "...other operating system",
:>
:>investigated and saw "Digital Unix". Thinking I would get a free version
:>of DU

  As opposed to signing up for the media-cost-only Tru64 UNIX or 
  OpenVMS Hobbyist programs, and getting real kits and licenses
  and such?  (The Hobbyist programs are designed to get hobbyists
  cheap (non-commercial) access to OpenVMS and to Tru64 UNIX 
  operating systems.)

:>I chose that and booted up. The result was that the SRM doesn't even
:>appear!

  The usual approach is via the ARC or AlphaBIOS firmware switch,
  and selecting the SRM.  

  If you choose to try OpenVMS on this box, you must very carefully 
  follow the very specific and very special sequence that is documented 
  in the OpenVMS Freeware Multia directory area, and use the special 
  Multia SRM -- both are included in the Freeware directory on the 
  OpenVMS Freeware website, and referenced in the OpenVMS FAQ. 

  (Please don't "wing it" when switching over to the SRM for use with
  OpenVMS on the Multia, though you wouldn't be the first to try that 
  approach -- not reading the directions, that is -- and you would 
  correspondingly also not be the first to get it wrong... :-)  

  I do not know what SRM will work with Tru64 UNIX on the Multia.
  (If any, of course -- the Multia is/was supported only with 
  Windows NT operating system.  OpenVMS is not supported on it.)

:>The monitor is no longer displaying text (only a scrambled version of
:>whatever text is trying to be displayed.)

  I have no idea what happened here -- I would guess that the version
  of Linux detected something else on the disks, and that the system
  was simply not booted with the correct console to get this other
  operating system (Tru64 UNIX?) to bootstrap.

