    Usually the syntax is sh -S 6/pst-t or just t 6. You might try t 6/? and
see if there are any optional parameters you can append to individual tests
that could further pinpoint things.

> ?TFL: 6/ram [PMAD-AA] (3: adrs=0xbf800018, rd=0xaaaaaaea,
> xpct=0xaaaaaaaa)
> ?TFL: 6/int-lb [PMAD-AA] (7: CRC=0xf1fc6f89, xpctd=0xbcf483a1)
> 
> I assume that there is a stuck bit on the network adapter RAM due to a
> faulty chip, am I right?

    It would appear to be failing some sort of RAM test with an unexpected
(incorrect) CRC.

> Could this be the cause for the problem netbooting via
> bootp/tftp from a Linux machine that gives following error message:
> 1430176?IO: 6/tftp, a.out err (-5)

    Assuming you were trying to boot an {E,X}COFF kernel and not ELF, yes.

> The bootp part works well, only when it tries to do tftp, it aborts with
> this message. The Linux log file says that tftpd was trying to send the
> correct file.
> Can the defective chip be easily located and replaced? I think it is the
> 62256 static

    Well if you enjoy soldering on motherboards (this chip is probably not
SMT so it could be worse), it shouldn't be too hard to find. Most of the
network hardware is located right up next to the bulkhead and around the
LANCE chip. 

> RAM with 32kb like it is found on most network cards. But what does the
> CRC error mean and where does it come from?

    I am told that every TurboChannel device has R3000 MIPS microcode
embedded into it that allows it to work at the PROM Monitor / SRM on both
MIPS and Alpha DEC hardware. All the tests and code are embedded into the
T.C. controller or on each option card. Pretty darn nifty if you ask me. The
only person who could likely tell you what that error message specifically
means is the person who wrote the MIPS code unfortunately, DEC didn't
document much from the MIPS platform. There is a T.C. reference on the DEC
ftp server though.

    Chris




