Alex van Denzel <bitbucket@adenzel.demon.nl> writes:
> I have (not so) recently aquired a Digital InfoServer 150. Since this
> (internally and externally) looked like a MicroVAX 3100, I tried some
> ways to get NetBSD running on this. The NetBSD user community eventually
> told me that the KA41-2 is quite something else than a KA41-A. Now I'd
> like to get this machine running in my network (TCP/IP) and like to know
> if this is possible, and how.

  If you can get the boot ROMs for MicroVAX equivalent, you should wind up
with something functionally equivalent.

  Normally the system ID is combined from some bits in the processor chip
(defining the particular chip implementation) and some bits loaded by the
boot ROMs (defining the model of system the processor is in). While I have
not done this with the Infoserver, I've been able to flip models between
the VAXstation 3100 M30/40 to M38/48, as well as converting between time-
share and server on the MicroVAX III, all of which use very similar CPU
chips, compared to the Infoserver.

  The ROMs are likely discontinued from DEC/Compaq, and if they're at all
like the VS3100 M30 ones, they're 40-pin word-wide ones, so a random EPROM
programmer won't be able to use them.

  However, if you have the source for the NetBSD CPU-specific code, you could
patch/modify it to treat any system model as being a MV3100.

  [Of course, doing this with a commercial operating system like VMS would be
a violation of the license terms.]

