In <39492E99.4FC4B5AB@apexxnet.com> "Jonathan Lee @ Apexxnet" <jlee@apexxnet.com> writes:

>All,

>   I've got a DEC 3000-400 alpha system with an old-style CDROM. It's a
>50 pin SCSI interface, and I'm wondering if I can replace it with any
>similar SCSI CDROM drive. This particular drive uses those old ROM
>caddys, and I'd like to swap it out for a more modern (not to mention
>faster) drive.

On DEC3000 you will need a drive that defaults to 512bytes/block in order to
be able to boot from it. I'd say everything DEC RRD4[4567] should work, but
generally also older Sun drives as they also have 512 b/b.

>    Also, can I drop in pretty much any new hard drive I want, or am I
>stuck with DEC hard drives only?

Generally yes. But a DEC OS will look at the vendor string of the drive
(inquiry data)

