I just got some interesting news from ADIC, maker of AMASS for Tru64 UNIX
and other OSes. I was asking about support for Tru64 V5.0, and was told that
Compaq is not willing to provide any support to ADIC in their effort to
support V5. ADIC has no recourse but to "tombstone" their Compaq/DEC UNIX
product since they need mfg. support to certify their products.

If you're not familiar with AMASS, it's a very large scale warm-store
filesystem, and I use it to store terabytes of MPEG files in a Sony tape
library. The AMASS product installs some kernel modules to provide it's own
filesystem to UNIX, hiding the all the usual tape library manipulation.

If what ADIC said is true, it really makes me wonder if it's time for me to
plan the phaseout of my GS140 running 4.0f and begin looking at other
platforms like Sun. I was looking to 5.0 for SAN/FC support, as right now I
have 1TB of ESA10000 on HSZ70s and I need to double that disk space in a
year or so. If I do that, there's nothing to keep me on *any* Compaq
platform...after all, Compaq might just decide to stop supporting other
semi-esoteric hardware that we use (like ATM adapters).

This is the first acutal instance of Compaq's new attitude that I've heard
of. I've heard rumors of this with Tru64 V5, but dismissed them.

Anybody got a different take?

Dave Schmitt
Chief Engineer, ITV
