John C. Coleman <johnc@tulane.edu> writes:
> We are planning to upgrade to a 19" monitor and a decent graphics card 
> that can that support up to 1280x1024 resolution in 16 or 24 bit color 
> with some 2-D and 3-D hardward acceleration and (if possible) OpenGL/GLX 
> support. Ideally, we would like to use Compaq's "True64" OS (already 
> have a license for it), but from our limited information it seems to 
> only support a few high-end graphics cards that all seem out of our 
> price range ($650 and up). Can anyone tell me if there are any cheaper, 
> VGA-based graphics cards (e.g., ATI Mach 64) that are supported by 
> True64 on this machine?

  The Alphastation 200 4/233 isn't exactly a screamer. If you try to push
more bits through the video subsystem, you'll likely reveal performance
limitations in the underlying system.

  Avnet has some DS10 packages that are somewhat attractively priced (they
used to be a lot better - about $1200 cheaper on the high-end box). See:
http://www.screamsonalpha.avnetdirect.com/custom/bundles/index.asp As an
.edu site, you can add this to the CSLG, or (if for personal hobby use)
the hobbyist program, so the fact that the Avnet boxes don't come with a
VMS/Tru64 license shouldn't matter to you.

  The ELSA GLoria that comes with that system is a bit of a slouch, and the
firmware intentionally doesn't support the older ZLxp-E2 card (it refuses
to init it in SRM mode, though if you switch to NT mode it comes right up).

  A ZLxp-E2 card does 24 bits and works in the AS200 you have. When I last
bought one (about 2 years ago) they could be had for $250. I'm unsure of
its 3D support, though. I think the difference between it and the next ZLxp
model is Z-buffer support, so I expect something in the family does 3D.

  The problem is that all of the mass-market high-end cards these days are
on the AGP bus, so Compaq can't use volume to drive the price down on their
PCI cards.

  Note that all my support comments are based on VMS, not Digital Unix, etc.

